# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses f4c9d13865f3, 2026-10-19
self_cause	one
prior_self	one
infinite	one
first	two	first	P6 C	first member of an ordered series of causes, i.e. the cause of the intermediate cause	absolutely first, uncaused efficient cause with nothing before it
intermediate	one
ultimate	one
# first: in S4–S6 "the first" is whatever stands at the head of a series and causes the intermediate; any chain one considers has such a member.
# In S7–S8 it is the uncaused cause that an infinite series would lack. This is the familiar objection, though Aquinas may intend the absolute sense throughout.
